"""Chapter 4 drill items — international trade and the balance of payments."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_4 = [
    q("International trade differs from internal trade mainly because", ["goods are always cheaper abroad", "factors of production are less mobile between countries", "exchange rates never change", "foreign goods are not taxed"], 1, "Factors are less mobile across borders: labour and capital cannot move as freely as goods, which is what gives countries different cost structures."),
    q("A country has an absolute advantage in a good when it can", ["produce it at a lower opportunity cost", "produce more of it with the same resources", "sell it more cheaply abroad", "produce it without any imports"], 1, "More output from the same resources — a productivity comparison. The opportunity-cost comparison is comparative advantage, which is a different question."),
    q("A country has a comparative advantage in a good when its", ["output per worker is higher than any other country's", "opportunity cost of producing the good is lower than its trading partner's", "exports of the good exceed its imports", "government subsidises the industry"], 1, "Lower opportunity cost. Trade gains come from what each country gives up, not from how much it produces per worker."),
    q("Nigeria needs 2 hours to produce a bag of rice and Ghana needs 3; Nigeria needs 4 hours to produce a bale of cloth and Ghana needs 3. Nigeria has a comparative advantage in", ["cloth", "rice", "both goods", "neither good"], 1, "Rice: one bag costs Nigeria half a bale of cloth but Ghana a whole bale. Ghana's advantage is in cloth, where it gives up one bag of rice per bale against Nigeria's two."),
    q("Using the figures above, the opportunity cost of one bale of cloth in Ghana is", ["1 bag of rice", "2 bags of rice", "half a bag of rice", "3 bags of rice"], 0, "Ghana needs 3 h for a bale of cloth and 3 h for a bag of rice, so the cloth costs it exactly one bag of rice. The 3 is the input requirement, not the opportunity cost."),
    q("Two countries can both gain from trade even if one is absolutely more efficient in every good, because", ["the less efficient country has lower wages", "each has a comparative advantage in something", "trade always raises prices", "governments subsidise exports"], 1, "Comparative advantage. The relatively less efficient country is still least bad at something, and specialising there raises total output."),
    q("The main gain from specialisation and trade is that", ["each country produces everything it needs", "world output rises as each country uses its resources where they are relatively most productive", "prices always fall", "employment always rises everywhere"], 1, "World output rises through specialisation. Self-sufficiency and guaranteed price falls are the protectionist's claims, not the gains from trade."),
    q("Which of the following is an argument for protection?", ["economies of scale in world markets", "protection of infant industries", "the gains from comparative advantage", "the wider choice available to consumers"], 1, "The infant-industry argument, that a new industry needs shelter until it reaches efficient scale. The other three are arguments for free trade."),
    q("An infant industry argument is weakest when", ["the industry is genuinely new", "the industry never becomes competitive and protection becomes permanent", "the country is large", "the industry exports"], 1, "The protection never ends. The case rests on the industry growing up, so indefinite protection is a confession that it has not."),
    q("A tariff is", ["a limit on the quantity of imports", "a tax on imported goods", "a subsidy to domestic producers", "a ban on trade with a country"], 1, "A tax on imports. A quantity limit is a quota, a subsidy is a payment, and a ban is an embargo."),
    q("A quota differs from a tariff in that a quota", ["raises revenue for the government", "limits the quantity imported directly and yields no revenue", "applies only to exports", "lowers the domestic price"], 1, "It restricts quantity and produces no revenue; the gain goes to holders of the import licence. A tariff raises revenue and also raises the domestic price."),
    q("The main effect of a tariff on an imported good is to", ["lower its domestic price and raise the quantity imported", "raise its domestic price and reduce the quantity imported", "raise the quantity imported without changing price", "leave both price and quantity unchanged"], 1, "Price up, quantity down. Domestic producers gain, consumers lose more than the producers gain, and the government collects the difference."),
    q("A complete ban on trade with a particular country is called", ["a tariff", "a quota", "an embargo", "a subsidy"], 2, "An embargo. It is a political instrument as much as an economic one, and its cost falls on everyone who traded with the banned partner."),
    q("Dumping occurs when a foreign firm", ["sells abroad below the cost of production", "charges the same price at home and abroad", "refuses to export", "is subsidised by its own government"], 0, "Selling below cost, usually to clear surplus or to destroy a rival industry. Subsidised exports are related but not identical, and equal pricing at home and abroad is normal trade."),
    q("Protection is likely to raise the price consumers pay because", ["domestic producers are always less efficient than foreign ones", "the tariff or quota reduces the supply of the cheaper import", "consumers prefer domestic goods", "tariffs are paid by consumers directly to government"], 1, "The import supply shrinks, so the domestic price rises towards the protected producer's cost. Whether domestic producers are less efficient is the reason the protection was wanted."),
    q("Devaluation means", ["a deliberate reduction in the value of a currency against other currencies", "a market-driven fall in a currency's value", "an increase in the value of a currency", "the abolition of exchange controls"], 0, "A deliberate official reduction. A market-driven fall is depreciation, and an increase is revaluation or appreciation."),
    q("Devaluation of the naira is intended to", ["make Nigerian exports cheaper abroad and imports dearer at home", "make exports dearer and imports cheaper", "reduce the volume of exports", "raise the external value of the naira"], 0, "Exports cheaper in foreign currency, imports dearer in naira. Reversing the two is the commonest error in the topic."),
    q("The trade balance improves after a devaluation only if", ["the country exports oil", "the sum of export and import demand elasticities exceeds one", "the government fixes the exchange rate", "imports are banned"], 1, "The Marshall–Lerner condition. If demand is inelastic both ways, the value of imports rises even as their volume falls."),
    q("The J-curve describes the tendency of the trade balance to", ["improve immediately after devaluation", "worsen first and improve later after devaluation", "stay unchanged after devaluation", "improve before devaluation"], 1, "Worse first, better later: contracts are already signed and buying habits change only gradually, so the first months usually show a deterioration."),
    q("The balance of trade is the difference between", ["visible exports and visible imports", "total exports and total imports including services", "the current and capital accounts", "government revenue and expenditure"], 0, "Visible goods only. Including services gives the balance on goods and services, and the current account adds income and transfers."),
    q("An invisible item in the balance of payments is", ["crude oil exports", "cocoa exports", "shipping and insurance services", "imported machinery"], 2, "Services are invisible because they cross no border as physical goods. Oil, cocoa and machinery are all visible trade."),
    q("The current account of the balance of payments includes", ["direct investment", "portfolio investment", "remittances from citizens abroad", "changes in the external reserves"], 2, "Transfers such as remittances are part of the current account. Direct investment, portfolio investment and reserve changes belong to the capital account."),
    q("The balance of payments always balances because", ["exports always equal imports", "a current-account deficit is matched by a capital-account surplus", "governments balance their budgets", "the exchange rate is fixed"], 1, "Every transaction is recorded twice, so the two accounts must sum to zero. That does not mean any component is in balance."),
    q("A country with a current-account deficit of ₦9 billion and capital inflows of ₦12 billion has", ["an overall balance of payments deficit of ₦21 billion", "an overall surplus of ₦3 billion", "a balanced payments position", "a trade surplus of ₦3 billion"], 1, "Inflows of 12 against a deficit of 9 leave a surplus of ₦3 billion, which adds to reserves. The two figures are not added together."),
    q("Which of the following would improve a country's current account?", ["a rise in the value of its currency", "a rise in domestic inflation", "an increase in exports of services", "an increase in imports of consumer goods"], 2, "More service exports earn foreign exchange without any import content. A stronger currency, higher inflation and more imports all worsen the account."),
    q("A persistent balance of payments deficit is a problem because it", ["raises the exchange rate", "must be financed by borrowing, selling assets or running down reserves", "reduces the money supply automatically", "increases exports"], 1, "It must be financed, and financing cannot continue indefinitely. Reserves run out, lenders lose confidence and the currency comes under pressure."),
    q("ECOWAS is best described as", ["a common market in Europe", "a West African regional economic community", "a United Nations agency", "a trade agreement between Africa and Asia"], 1, "The Economic Community of West African States, founded in 1975, which aims at a customs union and eventually a common currency."),
    q("The main benefit of a customs union to its members is", ["a common currency", "free trade among members and a common external tariff", "a single central bank", "the abolition of all taxation"], 1, "Free internal trade with a common external tariff. A common currency and central bank go further than a customs union requires."),
    q("Nigeria's membership of OPEC gives it", ["control over the world oil price", "a voice in coordinating oil production and prices", "a guaranteed share of world oil exports", "exemption from import duties"], 1, "A voice in coordination. No single member sets the world price, and no cartel can guarantee a share of exports."),
    q("Terms of trade are measured as", ["the ratio of export prices to import prices", "the ratio of exports to imports by volume", "the difference between exports and imports", "the growth rate of exports"], 0, "Export prices over import prices, usually indexed. A fall means a country must sell more to buy the same imports — a deterioration."),
    q("A deterioration in a country's terms of trade means it", ["can buy more imports for the same exports", "must export more to pay for the same imports", "has a trade surplus", "has devalued its currency"], 1, "Export prices have fallen relative to import prices, so more must be sold to buy the same quantity of imports."),
    q("Which is a visible export of Nigeria?", ["banking services", "tourism", "crude petroleum", "insurance"], 2, "Crude oil is a physical good crossing the border. Banking, tourism and insurance are services, which are invisible trade."),
    q("An exchange rate is the", ["price of one currency in terms of another", "interest rate set by the central bank", "rate of inflation in traded goods", "ratio of exports to imports"], 0, "The price of one currency in another. It is a price, and like any price it is determined by supply and demand unless it is fixed."),
    q("Appreciation of a currency is likely to", ["make exports cheaper abroad", "make imports cheaper at home and worsen the trade balance", "raise the price of imports", "increase the volume of exports"], 1, "A stronger currency makes imports cheaper, which tends to worsen the trade balance, and makes exports dearer abroad, which tends to reduce them."),
    q("Which of these is a non-tariff barrier to trade?", ["import duty", "an import quota", "an excise duty", "a value added tax on imports"], 1, "A quota restricts quantity without a tax, which is what makes it a non-tariff barrier. The other three are fiscal instruments and therefore tariff-type measures."),
    q("The theory of comparative advantage assumes, among other things, that", ["transport costs are zero and factors are immobile between countries", "governments intervene to protect industry", "every country produces every good", "exchange rates are fixed"], 0, "Zero transport costs and immobile factors are two of its standard assumptions — which is precisely why the real world deviates from the simple prediction."),
    q("A country's external reserves are held mainly to", ["pay for imports and defend the exchange rate", "fund the federal budget", "pay civil servants' salaries", "finance state governments"], 0, "To cover imports and support the currency. Using reserves for recurrent spending is the classic route to a balance of payments crisis."),
    q("Which of the following is a capital-account inflow?", ["proceeds from cocoa exports", "a foreign firm building a factory in the country", "remittances from citizens abroad", "shipping charges earned abroad"], 1, "Direct investment is a capital inflow. Export proceeds, remittances and shipping earnings all sit in the current account."),
    q("A country that devalues and simultaneously has high inflation will find that", ["the competitive gain from devaluation is eroded", "exports become permanently cheaper", "imports become permanently cheaper", "the trade balance improves without limit"], 0, "Domestic inflation raises costs and prices, undoing the price advantage the devaluation created, which is why devaluation without demand management rarely works twice."),
    q("Trade liberalisation means", ["the removal of barriers to trade", "the imposition of higher tariffs", "the fixing of exchange rates", "the nationalisation of exporting firms"], 1, "Removing tariffs, quotas and other restrictions so that goods and services move more freely; it is the opposite of protection."),
]
